<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="math125-probability">
  <title>Probability</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      A campus arts festival records attendance at its film screening and poetry reading.
      What is the probability that a randomly chosen festival attendee went to the poetry reading?
      How does that probability change if we choose only from people who went to the film screening?
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="math125-basic-probability">
    <title>Basic Probability</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Outcomes, Sample Spaces, and Events</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A <term>probability experiment</term> is a process such as drawing a slip from a shuffled box.
          An <term>outcome</term> is one possible result of the experiment.
          The <term>sample space</term>, denoted by <m>S</m>, is the set of all possible outcomes.
          An <term>event</term> is a subset of <m>S</m>.
          A <term>simple event</term> contains one outcome, and a <term>compound event</term> contains two or more.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      A <term>theoretical probability</term> comes from a model of how outcomes occur.
      When the model gives each outcome the same chance, we can compute probabilities by counting.
    </p>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Probability with Equally Likely Outcomes</title>
      <p>
        If <m>S</m> is a finite sample space with equally likely outcomes and <m>E</m> is an event, then
        <md>
          <mrow>P(E) = \dfrac{\text{number of outcomes in } E}{\text{number of outcomes in } S}.</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Every probability satisfies <m>0 \le P(E) \le 1</m>.
        The impossible event has <m>P(\varnothing)=0</m> and the certain event has <m>P(S)=1</m>.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <example>
      <title>Choosing a Track for Campus Radio</title>
      <p>
        A radio producer draws one of six numbered slips uniformly at random.
        Slips 1, 2, and 3 list jazz tracks, slips 4 and 5 list classical tracks, and slip 6 lists a folk track.
        What is the probability that the producer draws a classical track?
      </p>
      <p>
        The sample space is <m>S=\{1,2,3,4,5,6\}</m> and the event <q>classical track</q> is <m>C=\{4,5\}</m>.
        There are two classical slips among the six equally likely outcomes, so
        <md>
          <mrow>P(C)=\dfrac{2}{6}=\dfrac{1}{3}\approx 0.333=33.3\%.</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </example>

    <p>
      An <term>experimental</term> or <term>empirical probability</term> estimates the chance of an event
      using its observed relative frequency.
      If 18 of the past 60 radio episodes ran over their scheduled time, the producer can use
      <m>18/60=0.30</m> as an estimate for the chance of an overrun on the next episode.
      A <term>subjective probability</term> is based on judgment, as when a curator estimates
      the chance that a collector will lend a painting.
    </p>

    <exercise>
      <title>Literary Magazine Submissions</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The editors received 9 poems, 7 short stories, and 4 personal essays.
          They select one of the 20 submissions uniformly at random for a mock layout.
          Find the probability of selecting a poem as a fraction and a percent.
          Which submissions count toward the numerator, and which count toward the denominator?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="math125-combining-events">
    <title>Complements, Intersections, and Unions</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Operations on Events</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>The <term>complement</term> <m>A^c</m> is the event that <m>A</m> does not occur.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>The <term>intersection</term> <m>A\cap B</m> is the event that both <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> occur.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>The <term>union</term> <m>A\cup B</m> is the event that <m>A</m> or <m>B</m> occurs, including when both occur.</p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Complement Rule</title>
      <p>
        An event and its complement together account for all possible outcomes, so
        <md>
          <mrow>P(A^c) = 1 - P(A).</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Addition Rule</title>
      <p>
        When we add <m>P(A)</m> and <m>P(B)</m>, we count their overlap twice.
        Subtract the probability of the intersection once.
        <md>
          <mrow>P(A\cup B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A\cap B).</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Events are <term>mutually exclusive</term> (or <term>disjoint</term>) when <m>A\cap B=\varnothing</m>.
        In that case, <m>P(A\cup B)=P(A)+P(B)</m>.
        For example, drawing slip 4 and drawing slip 5 are mutually exclusive events on a single draw.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
      The attendance counts below cover 120 festival attendees.
      Select one attendee uniformly at random.
      Let <m>F</m> be the event that the attendee went to the film screening and let <m>R</m> be the event
      that the attendee went to the poetry reading.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="math125-festival-attendance">
      <title>Campus Arts Festival Attendance</title>
      <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" valign="middle"
               top="medium" left="medium" bottom="medium" right="medium">
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell>Film screening</cell>
          <cell>Attended reading</cell>
          <cell>Did not attend reading</cell>
          <cell>Total</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Attended</cell>
          <cell>18</cell>
          <cell>30</cell>
          <cell>48</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="medium">
          <cell>Did not attend</cell>
          <cell>12</cell>
          <cell>60</cell>
          <cell>72</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Total</cell>
          <cell>30</cell>
          <cell>90</cell>
          <cell>120</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <example>
      <title>Attending Both, At Least One, or Neither</title>
      <p>
        The intersection uses the cell for attendees who went to both events.
        Its probability is <m>P(F\cap R)=\dfrac{18}{120}=\dfrac{3}{20}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        To find the probability of attending at least one event, use the film and reading totals and subtract the overlap.
        <md>
          <mrow>P(F\cup R)=\dfrac{48}{120}+\dfrac{30}{120}-\dfrac{18}{120}=\dfrac{60}{120}=\dfrac{1}{2}.</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        The complement of attending at least one event is attending neither event.
        <md>
          <mrow>P((F\cup R)^c)=1-\dfrac{1}{2}=\dfrac{1}{2}.</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </example>

    <exercise>
      <title>Attending Exactly One Event</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use <xref ref="math125-festival-attendance"/> to find the probability that the selected attendee
          went to exactly one of the two events. Which two cells contribute to your numerator?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="math125-conditional-probability">
    <title>Conditional Probability</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Conditioning on an Event</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The <term>conditional probability</term> <m>P(B\mid A)</m>, read <q>the probability of <m>B</m> given <m>A</m>,</q>
          measures the chance of <m>B</m> among outcomes where <m>A</m> has occurred.
          The event to the right of the bar is the condition.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Conditional Probability Formula</title>
      <p>
        If <m>P(A)>0</m>, then
        <md>
          <mrow>P(B\mid A)=\dfrac{P(A\cap B)}{P(A)}.</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        In a two-way count table with a uniformly selected individual, divide the count in both events
        by the total count in the group named by the condition.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <example>
      <title>Changing the Condition</title>
      <p>
        In <xref ref="math125-festival-attendance"/>, 30 of the 120 attendees went to the reading,
        so <m>P(R)=\dfrac{30}{120}=\dfrac{1}{4}</m>.
        Among the 48 filmgoers, 18 went to the reading, so <m>P(R\mid F)=\dfrac{18}{48}=\dfrac{3}{8}</m>.
        Among the 30 people at the reading, the probability of attending the film is
        <m>P(F\mid R)=\dfrac{18}{30}=\dfrac{3}{5}</m>.
        Reversing the condition changes the denominator in this example.
      </p>
    </example>

    <exercise>
      <title>Choosing the Denominator</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use <xref ref="math125-festival-attendance"/> for each probability.
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li><p>Find the probability that an attendee went to the reading, given that they skipped the film.</p></li>
            <li><p>Find the probability that an attendee skipped the film and went to the reading.</p></li>
            <li><p>Explain which group determines the denominator in each calculation.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="math125-independence">
    <title>Independence and the Multiplication Rule</title>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Multiplication Rule</title>
      <p>
        If <m>P(A)>0</m>, the conditional probability formula gives
        <md>
          <mrow>P(A\cap B)=P(A)\cdot P(B\mid A).</mrow>
        </md>
        For two draws, multiply the probability of the first result by the probability of the second result
        given the first.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <definition>
      <title>Independent and Dependent Events</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Events <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are <term>independent</term> if
          <md>
            <mrow>P(A\cap B)=P(A)\cdot P(B).</mrow>
          </md>
          When <m>P(A)>0</m>, independence means <m>P(B\mid A)=P(B)</m>.
          Events that fail the independence test are <term>dependent</term>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      At the festival, <m>F</m> and <m>R</m> are dependent because
      <m>P(R\mid F)=\dfrac{3}{8}</m> and <m>P(R)=\dfrac{1}{4}</m>.
      Two mutually exclusive events with positive probabilities are also dependent, since the occurrence
      of one makes the other impossible.
    </p>

    <example>
      <title>Two Theater Prompts</title>
      <p>
        An improv team has 10 prompt cards, 4 for comedy and 6 for drama.
        The team draws twice, with each available card equally likely on each draw.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>With replacement.</em> The team returns the first card and reshuffles before drawing again.
        The draws are independent because the cards on the second draw have the original probabilities.
        <md>
          <mrow>P(\text{both comedy})=\dfrac{4}{10}\cdot\dfrac{4}{10}=\dfrac{4}{25}.</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Without replacement.</em> The team sets aside the first card.
        If the first card is comedy, 3 comedy cards remain among the 9 available cards.
        <md>
          <mrow>P(\text{both comedy})=\dfrac{4}{10}\cdot\dfrac{3}{9}=\dfrac{2}{15}.</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Without replacement, drawing one card of each type can happen in two mutually exclusive orders.
        Multiply within each order, then add the probabilities of the two orders.
        <md>
          <mrow>P(\text{comedy, then drama}) &amp;= \dfrac{4}{10}\cdot\dfrac{6}{9}=\dfrac{4}{15}</mrow>
          <mrow>P(\text{drama, then comedy}) &amp;= \dfrac{6}{10}\cdot\dfrac{4}{9}=\dfrac{4}{15}</mrow>
          <mrow>P(\text{one of each}) &amp;= \dfrac{4}{15}+\dfrac{4}{15}=\dfrac{8}{15}.</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </example>

    <exercise>
      <title>Selections from a Museum Archive</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A museum volunteer draws two items at random from a box with 5 photographs and 3 posters.
          Each available item is equally likely on each draw.
          For parts (a) through (c), the volunteer draws without replacement.
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li><p>Find the probability that both items are photographs.</p></li>
            <li><p>Find the probability that exactly one item is a photograph.</p></li>
            <li><p>Use a complement to find the probability that at least one item is a photograph.</p></li>
            <li><p>Find the probability that both items are photographs if the volunteer instead replaces the first item and mixes the box before the second draw.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <conclusion>
    <p>
      Adapted for MATH 125 with new examples and exercises from <q>Probability,</q> pp. 279<ndash/>289
      of <url href="https://www.opentextbookstore.com/mathinsociety/">Math in Society</url>, 2.6,
      by David Lippman, with contributions from Jeff Eldridge and Online Stat Book.
